    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Let's look at something called a bifurcation.
                Suppose we have a system of two differential equations:
                <md>
                \begin{array}{l}
                    \frac{dx}{dt} \amp = \amp -2x + ay \\
                \frac{dy}{dt} \amp = \amp -2x \\
                \end{array}
                </md>
                for some unknown value <m>a</m>.
                Note that <m>a</m> could be positive or negative.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the equilibrium point assuming that <m>a \neq 0</m>.
                    Evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium point.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the eigenvalues for this Jacobian.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Depending on the value of <m>a</m>, the eigenvalues could be
                    real, or they could be complex.
                    For what values of <m>a</m> are the eigenvalues real?
                    For what values of <m>a</m> are the eigenvalues complex?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If the eigenvalues are real, what type(s) of equilibrium
                    point do you have?
                    (Sink, source, saddle, ....) And will the solutions to the
                    system, <m>x(t)</m> and <m>y(t)</m>, oscillate over time?
                    Explain.
                    (Note: Be careful with values of <m>a</m>...
                    remember <m>a</m> could be positive or negative...
                    do the eigenvalue(s) change signs for different values of
                    <m>a</m>?)
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If the eigenvalues are complex, what type of equilibrium
                    point do you have?
                    (Sink, source, saddle, ....) And will the solutions to the
                    system, <m>x(t)</m> and <m>y(t)</m>, oscillate over time?
                    Explain.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <p>
        So our solution is divided into different parts, or "branches", based on
        the value of <m>a</m>.
        Up until a certain value of <m>a</m>, solutions will behave one way, and
        past that certain value of <m>a</m> solutions will behave a different
        way.
        This is called a "bifurcation."
    </p>
</worksheet>